  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Tote Services has $308M in contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Tote Services received 289 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $1.1M. Tote Services obligations are up 22.9% year over year. Tote Services obligations by fiscal year show earlier peaks and a recovery: FY21 $726M, FY22 $431M, FY23 $157M, FY24 $207M, FY25 $251M, FY26 $308M. Tote Services largest buying agency in FY2026 to date is the Department of Transportation with $254M in obligations, followed by the Department of Defense with $54.8M. Tote Services largest market is Deep Sea Freight Transportation with $269M in obligations. Tote Services also has obligations in Ship Building and Repairing totaling $39.4M and in Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) totaling $500. This profile reflects concentrated revenue from transportation-related work and a smaller presence in defense and R&D markets. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Tote Services' main federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Transportation is the main buyer, obligating $254M to Tote Services in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense is the next largest buyer, obligating $54.8M. Other agencies are not listed among the top buyers for FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where does Tote Services earn most of its obligations?",
        "body": "Deep Sea Freight Transportation is the largest market for Tote Services, with $269M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Ship Building and Repairing accounts for $39.4M, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) accounts for $500."
      }
